day":"Medium","idp_source":"gtwo_kml_202611201721","idp_filedate":1795195298000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"Several broad areas of low pressure are located along a front that  extends from central Florida eastward into the Atlantic for several  hundred miles, and are producing disorganized showers and  thunderstorms.  One of the low pressure areas, near the central  Florida coast, is likely to remain inland without significant  development.  A second low farther east could acquire subtropical or  tropical characteristics while it moves generally northward to  north-northeastward, passing near or east of the mid-Atlantic coast  through the weekend.  1. Regardless of subtropical or tropical development, this large  weather system is expected to produce gusty winds, dangerous surf,  rip currents and coastal flooding along portions of the southeast  and mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week.  In addition,  heavy rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will continue  across portions of the northeast Florida peninsula today, spreading  northward over coastal portions of the Carolinas and the  mid-Atlantic over the weekend.  Interests along the eastern coast of  the United States should continue to monitor the progress of this  system.  Additional information on this system, including Gale  Warnings, can be found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National  Weather Service, as well as products issued by your local National  Weather Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...30 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...medium...40 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}